# .ai/: the AI operating system of this repository

Scaffolder added this folder to an existing project (`scaffolder adopt`). It does not change your code, your dependencies or your CI.
It gives people and AI agents one shared definition of "done" that is **checked, not claimed**.

```
.ai/
  sdlc.json               the loop's configuration: gates, setup, integrity, agent (agents may not edit it)
  sdlc  sdlc.cmd  sdlc.py the runner (standard-library Python 3.9+): ./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc, on Windows .\<%sdlcDir%>\sdlc.cmd
  baseline.json           which gates passed and failed on adoption day (made by `sdlc baseline`; commit it)
  secrets.baseline.json   secret-like strings that were already in the repository on adoption day
  context/commands.md     the commands this repository defines, and where each was found
  context/architecture.md a map of the code, drafted by `sdlc onboard` and reviewed by a person
  rules/conventions.md    what the code does by convention, drafted the same way
  rules/                  how to work here: short, imperative
  skills/                 step-by-step playbooks an agent loads for that kind of work
  prompts/                templates the loop fills in and sends to the agent
  specs/                  one folder per feature: prd.md + acceptance.json
  evidence/               run output: logs, results.json, report.md (gitignored)
```

## First time (about five minutes)

1. `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc doctor`: are the tools this repository needs installed?
2. `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc setup`: install dependencies. An existing `node_modules` or virtual environment is used as it is, never wiped.
3. `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc baseline`: run every gate once and record what passes today. Then **commit `.ai/` including `baseline.json`**.
4. `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc verify`: the command to run from now on. Right after the baseline it passes by definition.
5. (optional, worth it) `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc onboard`: an agent reads the repository and drafts `context/architecture.md` and `rules/conventions.md`, folding in any `CLAUDE.md`, `.cursor/rules` or
   similar guidance that was already here. The tool then checks every path the draft cites, every `npm run` / `make` / `sdlc` command it names, and that every convention points at code. It cannot check that
   the description is *right*: read it, correct it, set `Status: Reviewed`, and commit. Every later agent run reads these files first.

## What "done" means here

Existing code is rarely green, so "done" is not "everything passes". It is **nothing that passed on adoption day fails now**, plus every acceptance criterion of the feature at hand:

- A gate that passed at the baseline must still pass. If it fails, the change broke something: `FAIL`.
- A gate that already failed at the baseline is shown as `WARN` (known). It does not block and it is not the agent's job, unless the feature is about it.
- When a known failing gate starts to pass, `verify` says so. `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc baseline --tighten` locks the gain in, so it cannot fall back unnoticed.
- The baseline is per gate, not per violation: a gate that was already red can get worse without `verify` noticing. Tighten as gates turn green.
- **Record the baseline where you verify.** A gate that passes on your laptop but fails in CI (a missing tool, a different OS) would show up as a regression there. If CI is the judge, run `baseline` in CI's environment, or fix the difference first.

## Gates (what runs)

Defined once in `.ai/sdlc.json` (stages: `<%stages%>`). They are the repository's own commands; see `context/commands.md` for what each one runs and where it was found.
A failing required gate stops the run at the end of its stage (`loop.failFast`), so the agent gets one focused problem.

## Features: spec, then code, then proof

```bash
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc new user-login               # an empty spec to fill in (prd.md + acceptance.json)
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc spec requirements.pdf        # or: an agent drafts it from a document (.pdf .docx .md .txt); YOU review it
./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc implement FEAT-001           # an agent writes tests and code; the loop proves it; it never commits
```

`prd.md` lists acceptance criteria (`AC-001` ...); `acceptance.json` maps each to checks. Verification fails if a criterion has no check.
Check types: `cmd` (run a command, usually ONE named test), `file` (exists, contains), `http` (needs a running app, see below).
A check that cannot run here is reported as `SKIP`, never as pass.

## Run the app for facts (optional)

By default the app is not started (`runtime.mode` is `none`), so facts are `cmd` and `file` checks. To check HTTP behaviour against the live app,
declare how to start it in `.ai/sdlc.json` (a human decision: agents may not edit that file):

```json
"runtime": {
  "mode": "local",
  "targets": { "web": "http://127.0.0.1:3000" },
  "local": {
    "provides": ["web"],
    "services": [
      { "name": "web", "cwd": ".", "cmd": "npm run start -- --port 3000", "env": {}, "pathPrepend": [], "healthUrl": "http://127.0.0.1:3000/", "startTimeoutSeconds": 90 }
    ]
  }
}
```

Then an `http` check may use `"target": "web"`. (`"mode": "docker"` runs `runtime.docker` with its own compose project name, so it never touches your development stack.)

## The healing loop (`./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc loop`)

```
verify -> green? -> done (exit 0)
   +-> failed: same failure <%noProgressLimit%> times in a row -> stop, ask a human (exit 3)
               <%maxIterations%> iterations used                        -> stop (exit 1)
               otherwise: heal prompt + evidence -> agent (or you) fixes the CODE -> integrity check (exit 4 if protected files changed) -> verify again
```

Protected for agents: `.ai/sdlc.json`, `.ai/sdlc*`, `.ai/baseline.json`, `.ai/secrets.baseline.json`, `.github/workflows/`; while healing also `.ai/specs/`.
